<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>StatementDelivery.Crypto</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>StatementDelivery.Crypto</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ADDED WHEN ENVELOPE ENCRYPTION LANDED, as a fifth building block alongside the original four.
    It is purely additive - nothing that existed before it changed shape to accommodate it.

    WHY ITS OWN PROJECT rather than a folder inside Persistence or ServiceDefaults:

      * DataKey holds plaintext key material. An architecture test asserts that NO project outside
        this one references that type, and a rule of that shape needs an assembly boundary to be
        enforceable at all - "no other folder" is not something the compiler or Cecil can check.
      * The cipher is pure computation over streams. It has no database, no HTTP and no AWS SDK in
        its reference graph, which keeps it testable without any of them and keeps the tamper tests
        honest: they exercise the format, not a stack of adapters around it.
      * ServiceDefaults and Persistence both need it. A shared dependency that lives inside one of
        two peers is how a cycle starts.

    Dependencies point inwards: Crypto -> Domain, never the reverse. Domain still references
    nothing at all.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Domain\Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="AWSSDK.KeyManagementService" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.DataAnnotations" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
